  "rights": {
    "status": "public_domain",
    "architects": [],
    "built": 112,
    "note": "Roman aqueduct bridge of Segovia, late 1st – early 2nd century AD (after 112 AD by the latest archaeological evidence, under Trajan or Hadrian); builders unknown. 36 arches rebuilt in the late 15th century under the Catholic Monarchs (works led by Pedro Mesa, prior of El Parral); statues placed in the niches in the 16th century; restored 1992–2000 (Francisco Jurado). Monumento nacional 1884, UNESCO World Heritage «Old Town of Segovia and its Aqueduct» 1985."
  },
  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "OpenStreetMap way 1366085092 «Acequia del Acueducto de Segovia» (the axis of the channel, 962.7 m) and way 503134835 «Arcada del Acueducto de Segovia» (the outline, wikidata Q244947) (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL)",
      "url": "https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/1366085092"
    },
    {
      "title": "Acueducto de Segovia — Wikipedia (es): 75 single arches to Plaza de Día Sanz, 44 double arcades and 4 more single arches (167); 28 m at the Azoguejo; 1 % slope; pier sections 1.8 × 2.5 m at the top and 2.4 × 3 m at the base; the attic with the channel; the two niches; 36 pointed arches rebuilt in the 15th century",
      "url": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acueducto_de_Segovia"
    },
    {
      "title": "Aqueduct of Segovia — Wikipedia (en): dating, the inscription panel over the three tallest arches, the niches",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aqueduct_of_Segovia"
    },
    {
      "title": "Wikimedia Commons — «Aqueduct Segovia statue.jpg» (near-frontal: tiers, piers, arches, the panel and the niche, measured on the 6.0 m bay, ±5 %), «Aqueduct of Segovia 08.jpg», «Segovia acueducto vista frontal.JPG», «Panorama view of Aqueduct of Segovia in 2014.jpg»",
      "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aqueduct_Segovia_statue.jpg"
    },
    {
      "title": "PNOA orthophoto, © Instituto Geográfico Nacional de España, CC BY 4.0 — the shadows of the arcade: bays of 6.0 m in the double arcade and next to Día Sanz, 5.2–5.8 m further south-east, where the arches begin, the solid wall after the Postigo",
      "url": "https://www.ign.es/wms-inspire/pnoa-ma"
    },
    {
      "title": "Terrain: MDT05, © Instituto Geográfico Nacional de España, CC BY 4.0 (tools/terrain.py and a 5 m profile along the axis) — 1016.4 m at the start, 982.0 m at the Azoguejo, 1004–1006 m behind the Postigo",
      "url": "https://centrodedescargas.cnig.es"
    }
  ],
  "triangles": 33020,
  "notes": "Built by code (model_sources/Q244947_acueducto_de_segovia.py, tools/meshkit.py) with AI assistance. The whole elevated stretch along OSM way 1366085092: a solid wall for the first 180 m from the south-east, 75 single arches up to the bend of Plaza de Día Sanz (bays growing from 4.6 to 6.0 m; the first 36 pointed, as rebuilt in the 15th century), the 44 double arcades across the Plaza del Azoguejo (bays 6.0 m; lower piers 2.0 × 2.6 m at the top stepping out every 3.6 m, lower arches 4.0 m; string course; upper piers 1.7 × 2.2 m, upper arches 4.3 m), 4 single arches over the rock of the Postigo del Consuelo, then the solid wall to where the channel goes underground. On top, the attic with the open specus. Over the three tallest arches the panel of the lost bronze inscription and the two niches on the central pier, the Virgin (resin replica) on the west-south-west face, the other niche empty. The top of the channel is a straight 1.005 % slope (1018.0 m at the start, 1010.0 m at the Azoguejo = 28 m over the square) and every pier goes 3 m below the MDT05 ground under it. Uncertain: the exact pier positions in the single stretch (the counts follow Wikipedia, the spacing the orthophoto, which shows fewer and wider bays than 75 would need), which arches are the pointed ones, where the 4 last single arches stand, and heights ±0.5 m. Granite colour by vertex, no ashlar joints. ground: keep with ground_fit false (it stands over streets and squares)."
